--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -20,4 +20,21 @@ followed by `0` or `1`) or when it does not resolve: a missing member, an array
 range or not canonical (`01`, `-1`), the `-` token, or a step through a primitive. Only own members
 resolve, so `/toString` and `/__proto__` fail on a plain object.
 
+## JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, applies a JSON Patch (an array of `add`,
+`remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test` operations) to `document` and returns the resulting
+document.
+
+```ts
+applyPatch({ foo: "bar" }, [{ op: "add", path: "/baz", value: "qux" }]); // { foo: "bar", baz: "qux" }
+applyPatch({ a: [1, 2] }, [{ op: "add", path: "/a/-", value: 3 }]); // { a: [1, 2, 3] }
+```
+
+Paths are JSON Pointers (see above). The input is never mutated and the result shares no structure
+with the document or the patch. Operations apply in order, and the patch is atomic: it throws an
+`Error` when the patch is invalid (not an array, unknown `op`, missing or non-string `path`/`from`,
+missing `value`) or when an operation fails (a missing target, an array index out of range, a failed
+`test`, removing the whole document, moving a value into its own child).
+
 Run the tests with `npm test` (Node 22.18 or later, which runs the TypeScript sources directly).
